\begin_layout Title
Other Regarding Preferences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
November 7, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In microeconomic theory, we are concerned with the set of assumptions related
 to individual preferences and the ordering of alternatives based on the
 degree of utility they provide.
 When rational agents maximize their preferences, this results in an optimal
 choice.
 This theory of choice serves as a background for demand analysis.
 However, economists have tried to gain insight into situations and behaviors
 that imply people are not acting self-interested.
 Today, researchers have begun to develop formal models of social preferences
 that assume people are self-interested, but are also concerned about the
 payoffs of others.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Fehr and Scmidt (1999) analyzed theories of fairness, competition, and cooperati
on.
 The authors attempted to explain why people exploit bargaining power in
 compeititve markets, but not in bilateral bargaining situations.
 They also wished to answer why cooperation is still maintained even when
 punishment is costly in voluntary cooperation games with free-riding opportunit
ies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Andreoni and Miller (2002) ran experiments in order to test the consistency
 of preferences for altruism.
 The authors wanted to see if the phenomena of altruism and fairness could
 be explained through a well-haved preference ordering.
 They applied the axioms of revealed preference to the altruistic actions
 of subjects.
 If the subjects satisfied the axioms, such as GARP, then it is implied
 that a utility function generated the choices.
 In the experiment, subjects were offered several opportunities to share
 suprlus with an anonymous subject, while the costs and income of sharing
 varies across decisions.
 Charness and Rabin (2002), on the other hand, try to create a new, more
 complicated model that better explains the patterns of nonself-interested
 behavior.
 They fit the evidence they obtained to a simple model in which the existing
 models reported in previous findings become special cases.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Engelmann and Strobel (2004) used smple one-shot distribution experiments
 to compare the relative importance of efficiency concerns, maximin preferences,
 and inequality aversion.
 In addition, they studied and compared the performance of two competing
 theories on fariness.
 The authors find that efficiency concerns and maximin preferences are important
 in simple distribution experiments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, Fisman, Kariv, and Markovits (2007) advance the research on individual
 preferences for giving, especially in regards to the results presented
 in Andreoni and Miller (2002).
 As in the earlier paper, the authors tested for consistency with utility
 maximization using revealed preference axioms.
 Then, in order to distinguish between preferences for giving and social
 preferences, the authors run three-person dictator games that vary the
 prices of giving.
 Finally, the authors have subjects face step-shaped budget sets in order
 to study decisions that are compatible with less well-behaved cases.
\end_layout

